T1	Quotation 11 17	"TOM!"
T2	Quotation 29 35	"TOM!"
T3	Quotation 47 95	"What's gone with that boy,  I wonder? You TOM!"
T4	Quotation 571 614	"Well, I lay if I get hold of you I'll -- "
T5	Quotation 809 848	"I never did see the beat of that boy!"
T6	Quotation 1062 1076	"Y-o-u-u TOM!"
T7	Quotation 1216 1290	"There! I might 'a' thought of that closet. What you been doing in there?"
T8	Quotation 1291 1301	"Nothing."
T9	Quotation 1302 1376	"Nothing! Look at your hands. And look at your mouth. What IS that truck?"
T10	Quotation 1377 1398	"I don't know, aunt."
T11	Quotation 1399 1534	"Well, I know. It's jam-- that's what it is. Forty times I've said if you didn't let that jam alone I'd skin you. Hand me that switch."
T12	Quotation 1593 1621	"My! Look behind you, aunt!"
T13	Quotation 1855 3295	"Hang the boy, can't I never learn anything? Ain't he played me tricks enough like that for me to be looking out for him by this time? But old fools is the biggest fools there is. Can't learn an old dog new tricks, as the saying is. But my goodness, he never plays them alike, two days, and how is a body to know what's coming? He 'pears to know just how long he can torment me before I get my dander up, and he knows if he can make out to put me off for a minute or make me laugh, it's all down again and I can't hit him a lick. I ain't doing my duty by that boy, and that's the Lord's truth, goodness knows. Spare the rod and spile the child, as the Good Book says. I'm a laying up sin and suffering for us both, I know. He's full of the Old Scratch, but laws-a-me! he's my own dead sister's boy, poor thing, and I ain't got the heart to lash him, somehow. Every time I let him off, my conscience does hurt me so, and every time I hit him my old heart most breaks. Well-a-well, man that is born of woman is of few days and full of trouble, as the Scripture says, and I reckon it's so. He'll play hookey this evening, * and [* Southwestern for "afternoon"] I'll just be obleeged to make him work, to-morrow, to punish him. It's mighty hard to make him work Saturdays, when all the boys is having holiday, but he hates work more than he hates anything else, and I've GOT to do some of my duty by him, or I'll be the ruination of the child."
T14	Quotation 4193 4242	"Tom, it was middling warm in school, warn't it?"
T15	Quotation 4243 4251	"Yes'm."
T16	Quotation 4252 4279	"Powerful warm, warn't it?"
T17	Quotation 4280 4288	"Yes'm."
T18	Quotation 4289 4332	"Didn't you want to go in a-swimming, Tom?"
T19	Quotation 4474 4503	"No'm-- well, not very much."
T20	Quotation 4570 4607	"But you ain't too warm now, though."
