T213	Quotation 73899 74487	"Aha! Talk about trying to cure warts with spunk-water such a blame fool way as that! Why, that ain't a-going to do any good. You got to go all by yourself, to the middle of the woods, where you know there's a spunk-water stump, and just as it's midnight you back up against the stump and jam your hand in and say: 'Barley-corn, barley-corn, injun-meal shorts, Spunk-water, spunk-water, swaller these warts,' and then walk away quick, eleven steps, with your eyes shut, and then turn around three times and walk home without speaking to anybody.  Because if you speak the charm's busted."
T214	Quotation 74488 74564	"Well, that sounds like a good way; but that ain't the way Bob Tanner done."
T215	Quotation 74565 74890	"No, sir, you can bet he didn't, becuz he's the wartiest boy in this town; and he wouldn't have a wart on him if he'd knowed how to work spunk-water. I've took off thousands of warts off of my hands that way, Huck. I play with frogs so much that I've always got considerable many warts. Sometimes I take 'em off with a bean."
T216	Quotation 74891 74926	"Yes, bean's good. I've done that."
T217	Quotation 74927 74955	"Have you? What's your way?"
T218	Quotation 74956 75410	"You take and split the bean, and cut the wart so as to get some blood, and then you put the blood on one piece of the bean and take and dig a hole and bury it 'bout midnight at the crossroads in the dark of the moon, and then you burn up the rest of the bean. You see that piece that's got the blood on it will keep drawing and drawing, trying to fetch the other piece to it, and so that helps the blood to draw the wart, and pretty soon off she comes."
T219	Quotation 75411 75684	"Yes, that's it, Huck-- that's it; though when you're burying it if you say 'Down bean; off wart; come no more to bother me!' it's better. That's the way Joe Harper does, and he's been nearly to Coonville and most everywheres. But say-- how do you cure 'em with dead cats?"
T220	Quotation 75685 76145	"Why, you take your cat and go and get in the graveyard 'long about midnight when somebody that was wicked has been buried; and when it's midnight a devil will come, or maybe two or three, but you can't see 'em, you can only hear something like the wind, or maybe hear 'em talk; and when they're taking that feller away, you heave your cat after 'em and say, 'Devil follow corpse, cat follow devil, warts follow cat, I'm done with ye!' That'll fetch ANY wart."
T221	Quotation 76146 76186	"Sounds right. D'you ever try it, Huck?"
T222	Quotation 76187 76224	"No, but old Mother Hopkins told me."
T223	Quotation 76225 76286	"Well, I reckon it's so, then. Becuz they say she's a witch."
T224	Quotation 76287 76575	"Say! Why, Tom, I KNOW she is. She witched pap. Pap says so his own self. He come along one day, and he see she was a-witching him, so he took up a rock, and if she hadn't dodged, he'd a got her. Well, that very night he rolled off'n a shed wher' he was a layin drunk, and broke his arm."
T225	Quotation 76576 76636	"Why, that's awful. How did he know she was a-witching him?"
T226	Quotation 76637 76833	"Lord, pap can tell, easy. Pap says when they keep looking at you right stiddy, they're a-witching you. Specially if they mumble. Becuz when they mumble they're saying the Lord's Prayer backards."
T227	Quotation 76834 76878	"Say, Hucky, when you going to try the cat?"
T228	Quotation 76879 76946	"To-night. I reckon they'll come after old Hoss Williams to-night."
T229	Quotation 76947 77014	"But they buried him Saturday. Didn't they get him Saturday night?"
T230	Quotation 77015 77162	"Why, how you talk! How could their charms work till midnight?-- and THEN it's Sunday. Devils don't slosh around much of a Sunday, I don't reckon."
T231	Quotation 77163 77219	"I never thought of that. That's so. Lemme go with you?"
T232	Quotation 77220 77254	"Of course-- if you ain't afeard."
T233	Quotation 77255 77295	"Afeard! 'Tain't likely. Will you meow?"
T234	Quotation 77296 77515	"Yes-- and you meow back, if you get a chance. Last time, you kep' me a-meowing around till old Hays went to throwing rocks at me and says 'Dern that cat!' and so I hove a brick through his window-- but don't you tell."
T235	Quotation 77516 77628	"I won't. I couldn't meow that night, becuz auntie was watching me, but I'll meow this time. Say-- what's that?"
T236	Quotation 77629 77650	"Nothing but a tick."
T237	Quotation 77651 77673	"Where'd you get him?"
T238	Quotation 77674 77693	"Out in the woods."
T239	Quotation 77694 77721	"What'll you take for him?"
T240	Quotation 77722 77763	"I don't know. I don't want to sell him."
T241	Quotation 77764 77810	"All right. It's a mighty small tick, anyway."
T242	Quotation 77811 77926	"Oh, anybody can run a tick down that don't belong to them. I'm satisfied with it. It's a good enough tick for me."
T243	Quotation 77927 78004	"Sho, there's ticks a plenty. I could have a thousand of 'em if I wanted to."
T244	Quotation 78005 78144	"Well, why don't you? Becuz you know mighty well you can't. This is a pretty early tick, I reckon. It's the first one I've seen this year."
T245	Quotation 78145 78190	"Say, Huck-- I'll give you my tooth for him."
T246	Quotation 78191 78205	"Less see it."
T247	Quotation 78342 78359	"Is it genuwyne?"
T248	Quotation 78403 78421	"Well, all right,"
T249	Quotation 78440 78455	"it's a trade."
T250	Quotation 78973 78989	"Thomas Sawyer!"
T251	Quotation 79060 79066	"Sir!"
T252	Quotation 79067 79126	"Come up here. Now, sir, why are you late again, as usual?"
T253	Quotation 79382 79424	"I STOPPED TO TALK WITH HUCKLEBERRY FINN!"
T254	Quotation 79587 79608	"You-- you did what?"
T255	Quotation 79684 79831	"Thomas Sawyer, this is the most astounding confession I have ever listened to. No mere ferule will answer for this offence. Take off your jacket."
T256	Quotation 79609 79649	"Stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn."
T257	Quotation 79949 80021	"Now, sir, go and sit with the girls! And let this be a warning to you."
T259	Quotation 81505 81521	"Let me see it."
T260	Quotation 81803 81828	"It's nice-- make a man."
T261	Quotation 82024 82074	"It's a beautiful man-- now make me coming along."
T262	Quotation 82207 82250	"It's ever so nice -- I wish I could draw."
T263	Quotation 82279 82296	"I'll learn you."
T264	Quotation 82251 82263	"It's easy,"
T265	Quotation 82297 82318	"Oh, will you? When?"
T266	Quotation 82319 82355	"At noon. Do you go home to dinner?"
T267	Quotation 82356 82380	"I'll stay if you will."
T268	Quotation 82381 82423	"Good-- that's a whack. What's your name?"
T269	Quotation 82424 82487	"Becky Thatcher. What's yours? Oh, I know. It's Thomas Sawyer."
T270	Quotation 82488 82572	"That's the name they lick me by. I'm Tom when I'm good. You call me Tom, will you?"
T271	Quotation 82573 82579	"Yes."
T272	Quotation 82725 82749	"Oh, it ain't anything."
T273	Quotation 82750 82762	"Yes it is."
T274	Quotation 82763 82800	"No it ain't. You don't want to see."
T275	Quotation 82801 82840	"Yes I do, indeed I do. Please let me."
T276	Quotation 82841 82855	"You'll tell."
T277	Quotation 82856 82906	"No I won't-- deed and deed and double deed won't.
T278	Quotation 82908 82967	"You won't tell anybody at all? Ever, as long as you live?"
T279	Quotation 82968 83012	"No, I won't ever tell ANYbody. Now let me."
T280	Quotation 83013 83041	"Oh, YOU don't want to see!"
T281	Quotation 83042 83081	"Now that you treat me so, I WILL see."
T282	Quotation 83266 83286	"Oh, you bad thing!"
